            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>AIR SEALING



During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Signifcant air leakage is coming in through the closet located in the hall that backs on to the ensuite bathroom and the kitchen. Given that pipe freezing issues were reported for that bathroom, it's likely that there is a signifcant puncture to the belly bag in this location and fixing this would both increase comfort for occupants, and reduce the likelihood of having frozen pipes. It can be fixed by sealing up the belly bag with a product like Blue Skin. Resolving this source of air leakage should be the priority.



** Through both bathroom exhaust fans. This indicates the dampers for the fans may be malfunctioning and should be replaced with new butterfly dampers that prevent back drafts.



** Around the penetration for the dryer hose. This connection could be sealed up with caulking.



** Through the old duct work for the kitchen exhaust fan. The exhaust fan in the kitchen has been replaced with a new recirculating fan, but the duct work has been left and is a source of air leakage. This ducting penetration could be removed, or simply blocked off as it's no longer used.



** Along the edge of the wall, where it meets the floor. This could be fixed by air sealing in the belly bag along the edges, or inside by adding caulikng along the margins of the trim. Sealing the belly bag would be the more difficult job, but better for comfort purposes.



** Around the penetration for the combustion air supply pipe for the wood stove.



** In the upper right hand corner of the front bedroom. Significant air leakage is occurring somewhere along this front wall, likely originating behind the bathroom tub insert and should be investigated further.



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a 20% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $130 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 5.1 GJ. 



There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

WALL INSULATION



MAIN WALLS



The main walls of your home appear to be 2x6 construction with batt insulation (R20). I have modelled adding 3 additional inches (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to the exterior (total R35) to bring your building up to existing new construction standards. Due to the large amount of labour involved in adding insulation to the exterior of your home, and the high cost, this recommendation should only be taken in conjunction with a siding upgrade.



Savings: By adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls, you could potentially save $360 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 15 GJ, and qualify for up to a $2,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</mainWalls>
                <foundation />
                <windows>WINDOWS



All of the windows in this house (9 total) are double pane, vinyl windows, that are original to the home. Some of the windows show signs of the seals being broken, as evidence by condensation between panes, and are in need of an upgrade. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of these windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



As your patio door is older, I have modelled replacing it with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified patio door rated for climate zone 3. 



Savings: By replacing these older windows and the patio door with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $310 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 13 GJ. 



There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>DOMESTIC HOT WATER UPGRADE



You currently have a 184 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified, instantaneous, condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $1300 per year in energy costs, slightly increase your annual energy usage by -0.2 GJ,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>FURNACE UPGRADE - PROPANE FURNACE



Your house is currently heated with an older 83% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified, high-efficiency, condensing, propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $400 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 18 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentiveprogram



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



SUPPLEMENTARY HEATING - WOOD STOVE



I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of oil currently saved) and the cost of wood in Yellowknife at the $420/cord "delivered" rate) if you continue to use your wood stove for about 50% of the heating requirement of the house.



Your stove is an EPA qualified wood stove that has a dedicated outside combustion air pipe and an efficiency of 77%. Wood stoves must meet either the Canadian Standards Association (CSA) B415.1-M92 (performance testing of solid-fuel burning appliances) or the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) wood burning appliances standards (40 CFR Part 60).



Savings: By using your wood stove for 50% of the heating requirements of the main living area of your house, at current fuel prices, you currently save $30 per year in energy costs. If you were to harvest your own wood you would see increased savings.



 







</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>SOLAR PV



SOLAR PV GENERATION:  Hydro Community



Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



Savings: By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the southeast facing roof of your house you could potentially save $940 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 11 GJ. 



There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program for electricity generation systems installed in a Hydro Community, including Yellowknife.

</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

          

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife



The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $0.91 per liter 

Propane - $0.61 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



